        ],
        "heading": "From church basements to 916 First Street",
        "image": "https://res.cloudinary.com/dsbllwpbh/image/upload/c_limit,f_auto,q_auto,w_1800/v1783025716/bataviail/pages/4cbc77b9-3054-4976-8573-7a7da4044200/building-916-first.png",
        "caption": "The permanent home — opened October 2025",
        "body": [
          "The pantry was born in a recession. In November 1981, after Batavia churches kept fielding the same request — help putting food on the table — the local Ministerial Association pulled congregations together around one shared cupboard. Interfaith wasn't a slogan; it was the founding mechanics.",
          "For four decades the operation grew the way Batavia things do: a donated storage building on Flinn Street, renovations by local businesses, a delivery van funded through the Lions Club, and — in 2007 — a switch to the Client Choice model, so neighbors shop for what their families actually eat instead of taking home a pre-packed bag.",
          "In October 2025 the pantry finally got a permanent home at 916 First Street: fourteen thousand square feet, triple the old space, funded by a $3 million community grant secured with help from Congresswoman Lauren Underwood. Same mission, much bigger table."
        ]
      },
